---
import ProductLayout from "../layouts/ProductLayout.astro";
import EvidenceShowcase from "../components/EvidenceShowcase.astro";
import { ArrowRight, Database, FileJson, ListChecks, ShieldCheck } from "@lucide/astro";

const description = "From PostgreSQL backup rehearsal to independently evaluated evidence: Nostekon workflows, semantic profiles, reports and explicit recovery boundaries.";

const layers = [
  { kicker: "Declare", title: "Drill specification", text: "A versioned file that names the failure scenario, the target, the restore location, how deep to verify, and your recovery time and data loss goals.", detail: "YAML in, strict validation, errors that name the field", state: "Available", later: false },
  { kicker: "Capture", title: "DrillRun evidence", text: "One JSON file with completion time, recovery phases, individual check results and, when you instrument for it, acknowledged writes and restored IDs.", detail: "JSON Schema, 16 MiB limit, up to 200,000 writes", state: "Format available", later: false },
  { kicker: "Evaluate", title: "V0 to V4 report", text: "Works out the deepest level passed, the first failed check, end-to-end recovery time, phase coverage and exact data loss from the ledger.", detail: "Go API, standalone Go command, downloadable report", state: "Available locally", later: false },
  { kicker: "Run", title: "Isolated PostgreSQL restore", text: "A disposable runner records successful PostgreSQL writes in a host-side ledger, deletes the source namespace after backup and restores into a separate k3d cluster. It checks the V3 row count and measures acknowledged-write loss at V4 against a zero-loss default or an explicit RPO budget. Permitted loss remains visible. Application-level instrumentation and general orchestration are still to do.", detail: "Two k3d clusters, pg_dump, bounded workload, measured RPO", state: "Lab only", later: true },
];
---
<ProductLayout title="Product" description={description} current="product">
  <section class="page-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The recovery evidence workbench</p>
      <h1>Nostekon</h1>
      <p class="lead">What you intended. What happened. What the evidence actually supports. Separate tools for running a rehearsal and reviewing its results.</p>
      <div class="hero-actions"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/demo/index.html#/suite">Explore Studio <ArrowRight size={17} /></a><a class="btn" href="/download/">Get the local app</a></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section product-flow"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">One workflow, distinct responsibilities</p><h2>Restore. Record. Review.</h2></div>
    <ol class="flow-track">
      <li><Database size={24} /><span class="flow-number">01 / Rehearse</span><h3>An isolated restore</h3><p>The Python CLI restores approved custom-format backups into a disposable database, or runs the fixed two-cluster lab.</p></li>
      <li><FileJson size={24} /><span class="flow-number">02 / Capture</span><h3>Original evidence</h3><p>Checks, timings and optional write-ledger measurements remain in versioned JSON, including failures and gaps.</p></li>
      <li><ShieldCheck size={24} /><span class="flow-number">03 / Evaluate</span><h3>An independent verdict</h3><p>The shared Go engine reviews the same original inputs in Studio, the local API and CI. It does not run the restore.</p></li>
    </ol>
  </div></section>

  <section class="wrap product-showcase" aria-label="Product evidence previews"><EvidenceShowcase /></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">Bring your own checks</p><h2>More than a successful backup job</h2><p class="section-lead">A matching row count can still hide nulls, duplicate IDs or orphaned references.</p></div>
    <dl class="facts">
      <div><dt>Author a semantic profile</dt><dd>Start from Orders or Inventory, then define not-null, uniqueness and foreign-key checks. Structure validation is not data validation. <a class="link" href="/demo/index.html#/profiles">Author a validation profile</a>.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Bind the rehearsal inputs</dt><dd>A restore plan binds the archive, image and snapshot expectations. Review original plan and run evidence together. Checksums bind bytes, not operator identity. <a class="link" href="/docs/guides/postgresql-backup-rehearsal/">Backup rehearsal guide</a>.</dd></div>
    </dl>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="wrap">
    <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow"><ListChecks size={15} />Under the hood</p><h2>Explicit formats. Shared evaluation.</h2></div>
    <div class="rows">
      {layers.map((layer) => (
        <div class="row">
          <div>
            <p class="row-kicker">{layer.kicker}</p>
            <h2>{layer.title}</h2>
          </div>
          <div>
            <p>{layer.text}</p>
            <p><code>{layer.detail}</code></p>
          </div>
          <span class:list={["row-state", { later: layer.later }]}>{layer.state}</span>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </div></section>

  <section class="section" style="margin-top: 48px;">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Why a separate cluster</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Restoring into a namespace of the same cluster shares that cluster's operators, CRDs, storage classes and secrets. That can hide exactly the dependencies you would lose in a real cluster failure, so Nostekon defaults to a separate cluster. The validator warns when a drill chooses the weaker namespace-only option.</p>
        <p><a class="link" href="/docs/concepts/isolated-restores/">Read how isolated restores work</a>, or <a class="link" href="/docs/">browse the architecture notes</a>.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ProductLayout>
